<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-xy-plane" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The <m>xy</m>-plane</title>

	<objectives>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				In this section, we learn how to:
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<ol>
			<li><p>.</p></li>
			<li><p>.</p></li>
			<li><p>.</p></li>
    </ol>
	</objectives>

  <subsection>
    <title>Why bother with the <m>xy</m>-plane?</title>

    <p>
    It is rare to have an application where we only deal with one quantity (one number) at a time.
    Often, the value of one quantity has a relationship to the value of another quantity.
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            For example, suppose you are driving on a road where travel at <m>60</m> miles per hour.
            If <m>x</m> represents the number of hours you have been driving and <m>y</m> represents the number of miles you have traveled, 
            then we have the relationship <m>y = 60x</m>. 
            Having an equation like this involving two variables <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> allows us to describe the specific connection between these two quantities.
            In this example, we will later see that the point <m>(x,y) = (31,1860)</m> is relevant for the equation <m>y=60x</m>, and this means that 
            having this point be relevant means that if you drove a total of <m>31</m> hours on a road trip, then you would have traveled a total of <m>1860</m> miles.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            As another example, suppose you need to track the height of a plant as it grows.
            If <m>x</m> represents the number of weeks since you planted the seed and <m>y</m> represents the height of the plant in inches, 
            the relationship between these two quantities might be described by the equation <m>y = 0.04x^2</m> mentioning both variables <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
            In this example, the point <m>(x,y) = (10,4)</m> is relevant for the equation <m>y = 0.04x^2</m>, 
            which means that <m>10</m> weeks after planting the seed, the height of the plant is <m>4</m> inches.
            The point <m>(x,y) = (30,36)</m> is also relevant, which means that <m>30</m> weeks after planting the seed, the height of the plant is <m>36</m> inches.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      The last example hints at the power of having an equation with two variables.
      We can plug in a number for <m>x</m> and get a number for <m>y</m>.
      But then we can plug in a <emph>different</emph> number for <m>x</m> and get the relevant <m>y</m>-value for this new <m>x</m>-value.
      The idea that each <m>x</m>-value leads to a different <m>y</m>-value means that this relationship can be described algebraically with an equation, but also geometrically with a graph on the <m>xy</m>-plane.
    </p>

  </subsection>

	<subsection>
		<title>The <m>xy</m>-plane</title>

    <p>
      The <m>xy</m>-plane (also called the Cartesian plane) has two lines (which are called axes) that are perpendicular to each other.
      The horizontal line is called the <em><m>x</m>-axis</em> and the vertical line is called the <em><m>y</m>-axis</em>.
      Each axis is labeled with the letter of the variable it represents: the <m>x</m>-axis is labeled on the right and the <m>y</m>-axis is labeled at the top.
      The two axes meet at a point called the <em>origin</em>, which can be labeled with the letter <m>O</m>. Here is a minimal picture of the <m>xy</m>-plane:
      
      <figure>
        <caption>Minimal picture of the <m>xy</m>-plane</caption>
        <image width="35%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw[white] (-2,2) rectangle (2,2);
                \draw[<->] (-2,0) -- (2,0);
                \node at (2,.4) {$x$};
                \draw[<->] (0,-2) -- (0,2);
                \node at (.4,2) {$y$};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>

      The axes divide the plane into four regions called <em>quadrants</em>.
      Quadrant I is the upper right region, Quadrant II is the upper left region, Quadrant III is the lower left region, and Quadrant IV is the lower right region.
      The axes themselves are not part of any quadrant.
    </p>

    <p>
      Once the axes are drawn, we can draw points in the plane.
      A point is communicated by writing what is called an <em>ordered pair</em>.
      An ordered pair is written in the form <m>(x,y)</m>, where <m>x</m> is the <em><m>x</m>-coordinate</em> of the point and <m>y</m> is the <em><m>y</m>-coordinate</em> of the point.
      For clarity, we will always write the <m>x</m>-coordinate first and the <m>y</m>-coordinate second (so note that these are in alphabetical order: x followed by y next), with a comma between the numbers to separate, and surrounded in parentheses.
      The <m>x</m>-coordinate is a number, and the <m>y</m>-coordinate is a number.
      So, when a point is written, we always write a left parenthesis, then a number for the <m>x</m>-coordinate, then a comma, then a number for the <m>y</m>-coordinate, and then a right parenthesis.
      For example, writing <m>(7,2)</m> is an example of a point, and for this point, the <m>x</m>-coordinate is <m>7</m> and the <m>y</m>-coordinate is <m>2</m>.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            The <m>x</m>-coordinate is the horizontal coordinate, since it tells us how far to the left and right the point is from the origin.
            If the <m>x</m>-coordinate is positive, then the point is to the right;
            if the <m>x</m>-coordinate is negative, then the point is to the left;
            and if the <m>x</m>-coordinate is zero, then we do not need to move to the left or right from the origin, so the point would be on the <m>y</m>-axis.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The <m>y</m>-coordinate is the vertical coordinate, since it tells us how far up and down the point is from the origin.
            If the <m>y</m>-coordinate is positive, then the point is up;
            if the <m>y</m>-coordinate is negative, then the point is down;
            and if the <m>y</m>-coordinate is zero, then we do not need to move up or down from the origin, so the point would be on the <m>x</m>-axis.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <principle xml:id="principle-point-requires-two-coordinates">
      <title>A point requires two coordinates</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Describing a point requires two numbers: the <m>x</m>-coordinate and the <m>y</m>-coordinate.
          The <m>x</m>-coordinate tells us how much to move horizontally, and the <m>y</m>-coordinate tells us how much to move vertically.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </principle>

    <example>
      <p>
        To plot the point <m>(7,2)</m>, we start at the origin and move to the right (because the <m>x</m>-coordinate is positive) a distance of <m>7</m>, and then we move up (because the <m>y</m>-coordinate is positive) a distance of <m>2</m>. This point is in Quadrant I.
      </p>
      <p>
        To plot the point <m>(-3,4)</m>, we start at the origin and move to the left <m>3</m> units, and then we move up <m>4</m> units. This point is in Quadrant II.
      </p>
      <p>
        To plot the point <m>(-6,-5)</m>, we start at the origin and move to the left <m>6</m> units, and then we move down <m>5</m> units. This point is in Quadrant III.
      </p>
      <p>
        To plot the point <m>(2,-3)</m>, we start at the origin and move to the right <m>2</m> units, and then we move down <m>3</m> units. This point is in Quadrant IV.
      </p>
      <p>
        To plot the point <m>(0,8)</m>, we start at the origin and do not move left or right (because the <m>x</m>-coordinate is zero), and then we move up <m>8</m> units.
      </p>
      <p>
        All five of the points we discussed are plotted in Figure <xref refid="fig-points-on-xy-plane"/>.
        We also included tick marks at <m>x=5</m> and <m>y=5</m> to help give a sense of scale.
        (Instead of providing so few tick marks, we could have provided a tick mark at each unit.)
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-points-on-xy-plane">
        <image width="80%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw[white] (-6.5,-6.5) rectangle (9.5,9.5);
                \draw[<->] (-6.5,0) -- (7,0);
                \node at (7,.4) {$x$};
                \draw[<->] (0,-6.5) -- (0,9.0);
                \node at (.4,9.0) {$y$};
                \node at (7,2) [circle,fill,inner sep=1.5pt]{};
                \node at (7,2) [above] {$(7,2)$};
                \node at (-3,4) [circle,fill,inner sep=1.5pt]{};
                \node at (-3,4) [above] {$(-3,4)$};
                \node at (-6,-5) [circle,fill,inner sep=1.5pt]{};
                \node at (-6,-5) [below] {$(-6,-5)$};
                \node at (2,-3) [circle,fill,inner sep=1.5pt]{};
                \node at (2,-3) [below] {$(2,-3)$};
                \node at (0,8) [circle,fill,inner sep=1.5pt]{};
                \node at (0.75,8) [above] {$(0,8.0)$}; % We have to move the label for this point to the right a little bit because it would otherwise overlap with the y-axis.
                % Tick marks at x=5 and y=5
                \draw (5,-.1) -- (5,.1);
                \node at (5,-.4) {$5$};
                \draw (-.1,5) -- (.1,5);
                \node at (-.4,5) {$5$};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
        <caption>Several points on the <m>xy</m>-plane</caption>
      </figure>
    </example>
    <p>
      One of the points we plotted above was <m>(0,8)</m>.
      Because the <m>x</m>-coordinate of this point is zero, we did not move to the left or right from the origin, so the point we drew ended up on the on the <m>y</m>-axis.
      It always happens that if the <m>x</m>-coordinate of a point is zero, then the point is on the <m>y</m>-axis:
    </p>
    <principle xml:id="principle-point-on-y-axis">
      <title>A point on the <m>y</m>-axis</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A point is on the <m>y</m>-axis if and only if its <m>x</m>-coordinate is zero.
          (Recall that the <m>x</m>-coordinate is the horizontal coordinate, and when <m>x=0</m>, there is no horizontal movement relative to the origin.)
        </p>
      </statement>
    </principle>
    <principle xml:id="principle-point-on-x-axis">
      <title>A point on the <m>x</m>-axis</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A point is on the <m>x</m>-axis if and only if its <m>y</m>-coordinate is zero.
          (Recall that the <m>y</m>-coordinate is the vertical coordinate, and when <m>y=0</m>, there is no vertical movement relative to the origin.)
        </p>
      </statement>
    </principle>

    <p>
      If we are asked to graph one or more points and they are all in the same quadrant, we can draw our axes in such a way that only the relevant quadrant is visible.
      For example, if all the points we are asked to plot have no negative coordinates, we can draw our axes in such a way that only Quadrant I is visible.
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Graph the points <m>(4,0)</m> and <m>(5,2)</m> and <m>(6,4)</m> and <m>(7,6)</m>.
          What do you notice about these points?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Since there are no negative coordinates, we will draw our axes in such a way that only Quadrant I is visible.
          (Our earlier graph showed tick marks at <m>x=5</m> and <m>y=5</m>. To show acceptable variations, this time we label tick marks at every unit on both axes.)
          <figure xml:id="fig-points-on-xy-plane-quadrantI">
            <image width="65%">
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw[white] (-0.5,-0.5) rectangle (9.5,9.5);
                    \draw[->] (-0.5,0) -- (7.5,0);
                    \node at (7.5,.4) {$x$};
                    \draw[->] (0,-0.5) -- (0,7.5);
                    \node at (.4,7.5) {$y$};
                    \node at (4,0) [circle,fill,inner sep=1.5pt]{};
                    \node at (4,0) [above] {$(4,0)$};
                    \node at (5,2) [circle,fill,inner sep=1.5pt]{};
                    \node at (5,2) [above] {$(5,2)$};
                    \node at (6,4) [circle,fill,inner sep=1.5pt]{};
                    \node at (6,4) [above] {$(6,4)$};
                    \node at (7,6) [circle,fill,inner sep=1.5pt]{};
                    \node at (7,6) [above] {$(7,6)$};

                    \draw (1,-.1) -- (1,.1);
                    \node at (1,-.4) {$1$};
                    \draw (2,-.1) -- (2,.1);
                    \node at (2,-.4) {$2$};
                    \draw (3,-.1) -- (3,.1);
                    \node at (3,-.4) {$3$};
                    \draw (4,-.1) -- (4,.1);
                    \node at (4,-.4) {$4$};
                    \draw (5,-.1) -- (5,.1);
                    \node at (5,-.4) {$5$};
                    \draw (6,-.1) -- (6,.1);
                    \node at (6,-.4) {$6$};
                    \draw (7,-.1) -- (7,.1);
                    \node at (7,-.4) {$7$};

                    \draw (-.1,1) -- (.1,1);
                    \node at (-.4,1) {$1$};
                    \draw (-.1,2) -- (.1,2);
                    \node at (-.4,2) {$2$};
                    \draw (-.1,3) -- (.1,3);
                    \node at (-.4,3) {$3$};
                    \draw (-.1,4) -- (.1,4);
                    \node at (-.4,4) {$4$};
                    \draw (-.1,5) -- (.1,5);
                    \node at (-.4,5) {$5$};
                    \draw (-.1,6) -- (.1,6);
                    \node at (-.4,6) {$6$};
                    \draw (-.1,7) -- (.1,7);
                    \node at (-.4,7) {$7$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
            <caption>Several points in the first quadrant</caption>
          </figure>          
          There are many possible things to notice about these points, but one thing that we might notice is that all of the points that we drew are on the same [straight] line.
          The reason that all four points are on the same line is due to another possible thing that we might notice: going from one point to the next, the <m>x</m>-coordinate always increases by <m>1</m> while the <m>y</m>-coordinate always increases by <m>2</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Distance between points</title>

    <p>
      Given one point <m>(x_1,y_1)</m> and another point <m>(x_2,y_2)</m>, we can find the distance between these two points.
      Because each point has an <m>x</m>-coordinate, we have labeled <m>x_1</m> for the <m>x</m>-coordinate of the first point, 
      and <m>x_2</m> for the <m>x</m>-coordinate of the second point.
      Similarly, because each point has a <m>y</m>-coordinate, we have labeled <m>y_1</m> for the <m>y</m>-coordinate of the first point,
      and <m>y_2</m> for the <m>y</m>-coordinate of the second point.
    </p>

    When two points have the same <m>y</m>-coordinate, the distance between the two points can be found by subtraction: take the larger <m>x</m>-value and subtract the smaller <m>x</m>-value.
    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                To find the distance between <m>(3,8)</m> and <m>(7,8)</m>, since the two points have the same <m>y</m>-coordinate, the distance is <m>7-3=4</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                To find the distance between <m>(3,8)</m> and <m>(1,8)</m>, since the two points have the same <m>y</m>-coordinate, the distance is <m>3-1=2</m>. Note the order of subtraction: <m>3</m> is a larger <m>x</m>-value than <m>1</m> is.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                To find the distance between <m>(3,6)</m> and <m>(-2,6)</m>, since the two points have the same <m>y</m>-coordinate, the distance is <m>3-(-2)=3+2=5</m>.
                Note the order of subtraction: <m>3</m> is a larger <m>x</m>-value than <m>-2</m> is. We still subtract the <m>x</m>-coordinates, even though one of the <m>x</m>-values is negative.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                To find the distance between <m>(-3,6)</m> and <m>(-7,6)</m>, since the two points have the same <m>y</m>-coordinate, the distance is <m>-3-(-7)=-3+7=4</m>.
                Note the order of subtraction: <m>-3</m> is a larger <m>x</m>-value than <m>-7</m> is. We still subtract the <m>x</m>-coordinates, even though both of the <m>x</m>-values are negative.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Suppose we have a first point <m>(x_1,z)</m> and a second point <m>(x_2,z)</m>. Note that <m>z</m> is the stated <m>y</m>-coordinate for both points, so the points would be drawn at the same height.
                We can subtract <m>x</m>-values to find the distance.
                If <m>x_1</m> is larger than <m>x_2</m>, then the distance is <m>x_1-x_2</m>.
                If <m>x_2</m> is larger than <m>x_1</m>, then the distance is <m>x_2-x_1</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>

    When two points have the same <m>x</m>-coordinate, the distance between them is the absolute value of the difference of their <m>y</m>-coordinates.
    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the distance between <m>(8,9)</m> and <m>(8,6)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Since the two points have the same <m>x</m>-coordinate, 
          the distance is <m>9-6=3</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the distance between <m>(8,9)</m> and <m>(8,16)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Since the two points have the same <m>x</m>-coordinate, 
          the distance is <m>16-9=7</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the distance between <m>(8,9)</m> and <m>(8,-6)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Since the two points have the same <m>x</m>-coordinate, 
          the distance is <m>9-(-6)=9+6=15</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the distance between <m>(8,y_1)</m> and <m>(8,y_2)</m>, under the assumption <m>y_2>y_1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Since the two points have the same <m>x</m>-coordinate, 
          the distance is <m>y_2-y_1</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the distance between <m>(z,y_1)</m> and <m>(z,y_2)</m>, under the assumption <m>y_2>y_1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Since the two points have the same <m>x</m>-coordinate, 
          the distance is <m>y_2-y_1</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>





    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Statement text       
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Solution text
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <!-- Try it -->
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Text
        </p>
      </statement>
      <!-- <response /> --><!-- Commented out the response area where a student can type. -->
    </exercise>

    <note>
      <p>
        Text
      </p>
    </note>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Title</title>
      <p>
        Text
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <warning>
      <p>
        text
      </p>
    </warning>

		<investigation>
      <idx><h>knights and knaves</h></idx>
      <idx><h>puzzle</h><h>knights and knaves</h></idx>
      <p>
        While walking through a fictional forest,
        you encounter three trolls guarding a bridge.
        Each is either a <em>knight</em>,
        who always tells the truth, or a
        <em>knave</em>, who always lies.
        The trolls will not let you pass until you correctly identify each as either a knight or a knave.
        Each troll makes a single statement:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          Troll 1: If I am a knave, then there are exactly two knights here.
        </p>

        <p>
          Troll 2: Troll 1 is lying.
        </p>

        <p>
          Troll 3: Either we are all knaves, or at least one of us is a knight.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Which troll is which?
        <idx><h>self reference</h><see>reference, self</see></idx>
        <idx><h>reference, self</h><see>self reference</see></idx>
      </p>
		</investigation>

    <!-- Strategy -->
    <computation>
      <title>Solving an equation with fractions</title>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>When does this strategy apply?</alert> 
              This strategy applies in any equation we are asked to solve when there is one or more fractions.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>How to apply the strategy</alert>
              <ol>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Step 1 text
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Step 2 text
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Take the least common multiple computed in the previous step and multiply both sides of the equation by this expression.
                    (Because we have to multiply the entire left side and the entire right side, 
                    it may be necessary to use parentheses due to the Order of Operations.)
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    On each side, distribute if applicable.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    In each term on each side, cancel where applicable.
                    This always results in an equation which has no fractions.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </computation>


    <!-- Try it -->
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Spend a few minutes thinking about the Investigate problem above.  What could you conclude if you knew Troll 1 really was a knave (i.e., their statement was false)?  Share your initial thoughts on this.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <response />
    </exercise>

		<definition>
			<title>Argument</title>
			<idx><h>argument</h></idx>
			<idx><h>premise</h></idx>
			<idx><h>conclusion</h></idx>
			<idx><h>valid</h></idx>
			<idx><h>sound</h></idx>
			<idx><h>proof</h></idx>
			<statement>
				<p>
					An <term>argument</term> <idx><h>argument</h></idx> is a sequence of statements, the last of which is called the <term>conclusion</term> <idx><h>conclusion</h></idx> and the rest of which are called <term>premises</term>.
					<idx><h>premises</h></idx>
				</p>

				<p>
					An argument is said to be <term>valid</term> <idx><h>valid</h></idx> provided the conclusion must be true whenever the premises are all true.
					An argument is <term>invalid</term> if it is not valid; that is, all the premises can be true, and the conclusion could still be false.
				</p>

				<p>
					An argument is <term>sound</term> provided it is valid and all the premises are true.
					A <term>proof</term> of a statement is a sound argument whose conclusion is the statement.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>

		<aside>
		<p>
			Our definitions of <term>argument</term>, <term>valid argument</term>, and <term>sound argument</term> are the same ones used in philosophy, the other primary academic discipline concerned with logic and reasoning.
		</p>
		</aside>

		<example>
			<idx><h>cookie</h></idx>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Consider the following two arguments:
				</p>

				<tabular>
					<row>
					<cell/>
					<cell>If Edith eats her vegetables, then she can have a cookie.</cell>
					</row>
					<row bottom="minor">
					<cell/>
					<cell>Edith eats her vegetables.</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
					<cell><m>\therefore</m></cell>
					<cell>Edith gets a cookie.</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>

				<tabular>
					<row>
					<cell/>
					<cell>Florence must eat her vegetables to get a cookie.</cell>
					</row>
					<row bottom="minor">
					<cell/>
					<cell>Florence eats her vegetables.</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
					<cell><m>\therefore</m></cell>
					<cell>Florence gets a cookie.</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>

				<p>
					(The symbol <q><m>\therefore</m></q>
					<notation>
					<usage><m>\therefore</m></usage>
					<description><q>therefore</q></description>
					</notation>
					means <q>therefore</q>)
				</p>

				<p>
					Are these arguments valid?
				</p>
			</statement>

			<solution>
				<p>
					Do you agree that the first argument is valid but the second argument is not?
					We will soon develop a better understanding of the logic involved in this analysis, but if your intuition agrees with this assessment, then you are in good shape.
				</p>

				<p>
					Notice the two arguments look almost identical.
					Edith and Florence both eat their vegetables.
					In both cases, there is a connection between the eating of vegetables and cookies.
					Yet we claim that it is valid to conclude that Edith gets a cookie, but not that Florence does.
					The difference must be in the connection between eating vegetables and getting cookies.
					We need to be skilled at reading and comprehending these sentences.
					Do the two sentences mean the same thing?
				</p>

				<p>
					Unfortunately, in everyday language we are often sloppy, and you might be tempted to say they are equivalent.
					But notice that just because Florence <em>must</em> eat her vegetables, we have not claimed that doing so would be <em>enough</em> (she might also need to clean her room, for example).
					In everyday (non-mathematical) practice, you might be tempted to say this <q>other direction</q> is implied.
					In mathematics, we never get that luxury.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>

		<remark>
			<p>
				The arguments in the example above illustrate another important point: Even if you don't care about the advancement of human knowledge in the field of mathematics, becoming skilled at analyzing arguments is useful.
				And even if you don't want to give your grandmother a cookie.
				If you are <em>using</em> mathematics to solve problems in some other discipline, it is still necessary to demonstrate that your solution is correct.
				You better have a good argument that it is!
			</p>
		</remark>



  </subsection>

